@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+---
+tier: spec
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: clarifying
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/app.py, src/flask/ctx.py, tests/test_appctx.py, tests/test_reqctx.py, tests/test_signals.py]
+---
+
+# exhaustive-teardown
+
+## Why
+An exception in one teardown callback or teardown-signal receiver currently
+prevents later cleanup from running and can prevent the context-local state
+from being restored.
+
+## What
+- Run every registered request- and application-context teardown callback and
+  teardown-signal receiver even when earlier cleanup raises.
+- Raise collected teardown failures only after that cleanup finishes, while
+  restoring the context-local state first.
+- Non-goal: change non-teardown signal dispatch or callback ordering.
+
+## How
+- Route teardown callbacks and signal receivers through one private runner that
+  collects `BaseException` instances in registration order.
+- Use an exception group on Python versions that provide it; retain the first
+  error on Python 3.10.
+- Ensure `AppContext.pop` restores its context variable even if request or app
+  teardown raises.
+
+## Alternatives
+- Let Blinker dispatch the teardown signals directly. Rejected because its
+  normal send path stops at the first failing receiver, contrary to D-17.
+
+## Impact
+- `AppContext.pop` is the common request/application context exit path,
+  including test contexts, WSGI dispatch, CLI use, and copied contexts.
+- `Flask.do_teardown_request` and `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext` are lifecycle
+  hooks used by that path; blueprint teardown ordering remains unchanged.
+- The affected public compatibility contract is teardown execution and error
+  reporting. No persisted data, permissions, or external services are involved.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Every request teardown callback and request teardown-signal receiver runs
+  when earlier ones raise, and their errors are retained — test:
+  `tests/test_reqctx.py`.
+- [x] Every application teardown callback and application teardown-signal
+  receiver runs when earlier ones raise, and their errors are retained — test:
+  `tests/test_appctx.py` and `tests/test_signals.py`.
+- [x] A failing teardown leaves no request or application context active — test:
+  `tests/test_appctx.py` and `tests/test_reqctx.py`.
+
+## Open questions
+None.
+
+## Decisions
+- application-lifecycle: teardown uses a shared exhaustive runner; direct
+  Blinker dispatch is rejected because it stops on receiver failures.
